---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---
<BaseLayout title="National Health Assurance: Phased Rollout">
  <main>

  <section class="card">
    <h2>Twelve years, sequenced by readiness</h2>
    <p class="desc">The plan defines a foundational Phase 0 followed by
      eight delivery phases. The dates are anchors, not automatic deadlines:
      independent readiness gates can hold, resize, or roll back a wave before
      the next major commitment. Select a milestone to see what is built and
      what evidence permits the program to continue.</p>
    <div class="tiles rollout-summary" aria-label="Rollout headline milestones">
      <div class="tile">
        <div class="label">Controlled roadmap</div>
        <div class="value">P0–P8</div>
        <div class="range">foundation plus eight delivery phases</div>
      </div>
      <div class="tile">
        <div class="label">Public default coverage</div>
        <div class="value">Year 8</div>
        <div class="range">after claims, capacity, rights, and continuity checks</div>
      </div>
      <div class="tile">
        <div class="label">Expanded benefits</div>
        <div class="value">Year 10</div>
        <div class="range">LTC, behavioral health, dental, vision, hearing, EMS</div>
      </div>
      <div class="tile">
        <div class="label">Maturity certification</div>
        <div class="value">Year 12</div>
        <div class="range">full integration, manufacturing, records, and payment</div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div id="rollout-timeline" class="rollout-timeline"
      aria-label="Interactive twelve-year phase timeline"></div>
    <div id="rollout-detail" class="rollout-detail" aria-live="polite"></div>
    <p class="note">Year numbers are relative implementation anchors. They do
      not assign a calendar start date or override a failed readiness gate.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="card">
    <h2>How the whole system moves together</h2>
    <p class="desc">Coverage cannot outrun identity and payment; cost-sharing
      cannot disappear before staffed diagnostic and specialist capacity
      exists; expanded benefits cannot outrun workforce and home-care
      readiness. The lanes below preserve all thirteen domain workstreams in
      the integrated roadmap.</p>
    <div class="tbl-scroll">
      <table class="data rollout-matrix" id="rollout-domain-matrix">
        <caption class="sr-only">Thirteen rollout domains across five phase bands</caption>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="card">
    <h2>The two largest physical buildouts</h2>
    <p class="desc">The delivery network and public medicines capacity start
      before national coverage conversion and keep scaling after it. Numeric
      unit-network thresholds are readiness floors, while the manufacturing
      sequence is capability-based.</p>
    <div class="buildout-grid">
      <div class="buildout-panel">
        <h3>Community diagnostic-treatment network</h3>
        <div id="unit-ramp" class="unit-ramp"
          aria-label="Diagnostic-treatment unit network scale-up"></div>
        <p class="note">Type A micro, Type B neighborhood, Type C rural
          enhanced, and Type D urban/public-health units. Phase 5 reaches at
          least 65% population coverage; Gate 2 requires at least 80% before
          broad cost-sharing elimination; maturity requires at least 95%
          within the access standard and at least 15,000 certified units.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="buildout-panel">
        <h3>Medicines, diagnostics, and public production</h3>
        <div id="drug-pipeline" class="drug-pipeline"
          aria-label="Public drug purchasing and manufacturing rollout"></div>
        <p class="note">The pharmacy claims utility, national purchasing,
          public manufacturing, formulary governance, shortage controls,
          supplier qualification, inventory visibility, and backup supply
          scale as one connected capability, not as a stand-alone factory.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="card">
    <h2>Who carries the rollout</h2>
    <p class="desc">The changeover deliberately separates transition command,
      daily operations and payment, and independent rights and verification.
      That prevents the organization being evaluated from grading its own
      readiness.</p>
    <div id="rollout-agencies" class="agency-grid"></div>
  </section>

  <section class="card">
    <h2>Eight gates that can stop the clock</h2>
    <p class="desc">A gate is a decision, not a date. If evidence is below the
      floor, the plan preserves care and payment, invokes a manual or
      legacy fallback, repairs the defect, and re-enters review.</p>
    <div id="rollout-gates" class="gate-grid"></div>
  </section>

  <section class="card">
    <h2>Protected transition work</h2>
    <p class="desc">The plan assumes $1.2–$2.0 trillion of incremental
      transition and implementation cost over 10–12 years, but explicitly
      does not invent phase shares or straight-line the total. Every dollar
      must attach to a costed work package, deliverable, owner, gate,
      contingency, and transfer into mature operations.</p>
    <div class="transition-envelope" aria-label="Transition cost envelope">
      <div class="transition-envelope-value">$1.2T–$2.0T</div>
      <div>
        <b>Cumulative transition envelope</b>
        <span>continuity, infrastructure, capacity, migration, stabilization,
          workforce, and wind-down, not mature annual operating cost</span>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div id="rollout-workstreams" class="workstream-grid"></div>
    <p class="note">A gate hold cannot cut mandatory care, medication,
      provider-payment, appeal, or continuity fallbacks. It changes the pace
      or scope of new deployment and triggers repair.</p>
  </section>

  <p class="rollout-source">
    <a href="https://github.com/mikehalvorson/US-National-Health-Assurance-System" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Sources and methodology</a>.</p>

  </main>
</BaseLayout>

<script>
  import '../scripts/rollout-client.ts';
</script>
